
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% The following kinetic scheme for N2-H2 mixtures
%- is based on references 
%   V Guerra, A Tejero-del-Caz, C D Pintassilgo and L L Alves
%    Modelling N2–O2 plasmas: volume and surface kinetics
%   M Jiménez-Redondo, A Chatain, L Vettier, O Guaitella, G Cernogora, N Carrasco, L L Alves and L Marques
%    N2-H2 capacitively coupled radio-frequency discharges at low pressure. 
%    Part II: Modelling results: the relevance of plasma-surface interaction
%- considers a vibrational description of N2(X,v), for v= 0-44 (NOT 59)
%- adopts a kinetic description for volume N2/N species, corrected according to T Silva comments (2024)
%- adopts a kinetic description for volume H2/H species, in course of updating
%- adopts a kinetic description for volume NxHy mixed species, probably to be updated (concerning the set of ion species)
%- adopts a mesoscopic description for the surface kinetics, with adjustments to resolve numerical issues

%%%% Version Scheme14 considers the following %%%
% - corrects the rate coefficient for reactions H2(X,v=0) + NH(X) + M -> NH3(X) + M 
% - corrects the stoichiometry of reaction NH2(-,X) + NH3(+,X) -> NH2(X) + NH3(X)
% - updates the cross section file "NH3_LXCat_extra.txt" (the previous file "NH3_extra.txt" contains an error)
% - uses the cross section files "H2_LXCat_extra.txt" and "H_vib_LXCat.txt" (a reorganization of "H2_extra.txt")
% - uses the cross section file "H_LXCat_extra.txt" (new version of "H_extra.txt" with the same content)
% - introduces missing outflow reactions for some species (e.g. some negative ions and some mixed species)

%%% SEVERAL MECHANISMS ARE STILL UNDER DISCUSSION / UPDATING: CHECK LLALVES2024 %%% 

% AND ADDITIONALLY 
% - it decreases by 10 (to 1e-17 m-3) the rate coefficient of reaction N(2D) + NH3(X) -> NH(X) + NH2(X)
% - it replaces the ionTransportQDB function with QGMTransport, 
%    using parameter "false" to remove the influence of ion collisions in the evaluation of mean-free-paths 
% - it completes / corrects the kinetic scheme for the reactions below
%    N2(A3Su+) + NH3(X) -> N2(X,v=0) + H(1S) + NH2(X) (same rate coefficient)
%    N2(A3Su+) + H(1S) -> N2(X,v=0) + H(1S) 			5e−17
%    N2(A3Su+) + H2(X,v=0) -> N2(X,v=0) + 2H(1S) 		2e-16 x exp(−3500/Tg)
%    N2(B3Pg) + H2(X,v=0)  -> N2(A3Su+) + H2(X,v=0)  	2.5e-17
%    N2(a'1Su-) + H(1S) -> N2(X,v=0) + H(1S)			1.5e-17
%    N2(a'1Su-) + H2(X,v=0) -> N2(X,v=0) + 2H(1S)		2.6e-17
%    H(+,gnd) + 2H2(X,v=0) -> 2H(1S) + H3(+,X)          3.1E-41 x (300/Tg)^0.5
% - it corrects the rate coefficients of the reactions below 
%    dividing them by 1e4 (this is a correction on units conversion)
%    in some cases, multiplying them by the thermal relative velocity between the two species (1.7821d2 for molecule-atom or 2.058d2 for atom-atom) 
%    in some cases (ion-ion recombination), simply updating the rate coefficient 
%     H(2S) + H2(X,v=0) -> e + H3(+,X) 
%     H(2S) + H2(X,v=0) -> 3H(1S)      
%     H(2S) + H2(X) -> H(2P) + H2(X)    
%     H(2P) + H2(X) -> H(2S) + H2(X)        
%     H2(+,X) + H(-,gnd) -> H2(X,v=0) + H(3)
%     H2(+,X) + H(-,gnd) -> H2(X,v=0) + H(4)
%     H2(+,X) + H(-,gnd) -> H2(X,v=0) + H(5)
%     H(+,gnd) + H(-,gnd) -> H(1S) + H(3)
% - it corrects/introduces the rate coefficients for electron-ion recombination
%     e + H2(+,X) -> H(1S) + H(2S,2P,3,4,5)              
%     e + H3(+,X) -> 3H(1S)                                      
%     e + H3(+,X) -> H2(X,v=6:14) + H(2S,2P)
%     e + H(+,gnd) -> H(1S,2S,2P,3,4,5)
%     2e + H(+,gnd) -> e + H(1S,2S,2P,3,4,5)
%     2e + H3(+,X) -> e + H2(X,v=0) + H(1S)                      
%     2e + H2(+,X) -> e + 2H(1S)                 
% - it updates the rate coefficients for electron impact excitation/deexcitation of H using Janev et al (p.11)
%     e + H(1S) <-> e + H(2S)                                   
%     e + H(1S) <-> e + H(2P)                                    
%     e + H(1S) <-> e + H(3)                                      
%     e + H(1S) <-> e + H(4)                                     
%     e + H(1S) <-> e + H(5)                                   
%     %e + H(2S) <-> e + H(2P)                             
%     e + H(2S) <-> e + H(3)                                  
%     e + H(2S) <-> e + H(4)                                   
%     e + H(2S) <-> e + H(5)                                 
%     e + H(2P) <-> e + H(3)                           
%     e + H(2P) <-> e + H(4)                                    
%     e + H(2P) <-> e + H(5)                                
%     e + H(3) <-> e + H(4)                              
%     e + H(3) <-> e + H(5)                                    
%     e + H(4) <-> e + H(5)                               
% - it updates the rate coefficient for electron impact detachment of H- using Janev et al (p.23)
%     e + H(-,gnd) -> 2e + H(1S)                                 
%
% AND ADDITIONALLY (THIS MODIFICATION WAS PUT "ON HOLD" FOR CS14)
% it replaces the physisorption/desorption mechanisms
%  N(4S) + wall_F(v) -> wall_N(F) | thermalEffusionSurface      | 1, 0, wallTemperature            | 
%  wall_N(F) -> N(4S) + wall_F(v) | thermalDesorption           | 1e15, 51e3                       | 
%  H(1S) + wall_F(v) -> wall_H(F) | thermalEffusionSurface      | 1, 0, wallTemperature            | 
%  wall_H(F) -> H(1S) + wall_F(v) | thermalDesorption           | 1e15, 37e3                       | 
% by the densities of wall_N(F) and wall_H(F), 
% obtained by analytically solving the rate balance equations for the previous reactions   
%  [wall_N(F)] = [N(4S)] * [wall_F(v)] * thermalEffusionSurface / thermalDesorption
%  [wall_H(F)] = [H(1S)] * [wall_F(v)] * thermalEffusionSurface / thermalDesorption
% and by modifying the surface transport reactions involving wall_N(F) or wall_H(F), 
%  using (e.g. for wall_N(F))
%   N(4S) + wall_H(S) + wall_F(v) -> N(4S) + wall_NH(S) + wall_F(v) | effectiveSurfaceTransport   | ten parameters |
%  instead of 
%   wall_N(F) + wall_H(S) -> wall_NH(S) + wall_F(v)                 | surfaceTransport            | five parameters |

% AND ADDITIONALLY (THIS MODIFICATION WAS PUT "ON HOLD" FOR CS14)
% it "forces the calculation" of the densities wall_N(F) and wall_N(F) by using (e.g. for wall_N(F))
%  N(4S) + wall_F(v) -> wall_N(F) + N(4S) + wall_F(v)                | thermalEffusionSurface      | 1, 0, wallTemperature | 
%  wall_N(F) -> e                                                    | thermalDesorption           | 1e15, 51e3           | 
 
% AND ADDITIONALLY
% it reduces by 10 the rate coefficients of H(2S) + H2(X,v=0) reactions
% it includes the VDF manifold in the excitation of N2(A,a',a,B,C,w)

% AND ADDITIONALLY (August 2024)
% it revises the data of mesoscopic surface model, to follow more closely the paper of B Gordiets et al 1998
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%---- E-V processes ----  
% for H2: includes B' and D1 state now.
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=0)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              | %H2(B1Su,C1Pu, B', D1) 
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=1)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=2)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=3)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=4)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              | 
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=5)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=6)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=7)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=8)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=9)                          | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=10)                         | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=11)                         | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=12)                         | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=13)                         | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              |  
e + H2(X,v=0:14) -> e + H2(X,w=14)                         | hydrogenIndEV                 |                                                              | 
                                                                                                                                                          
